  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 108 PM MDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThe long term period starting on Friday morning looks to begin with \nsouthwesterly flow over the forecast area at 500-mb, with ridging \njust to the east and troughing across the Western United States. \nSeveral shortwave troughs embedded in the southwesterly flow may \nimpact the forecast area during this time. Ensemble guidance is in \ngood agreement that this pattern is going to last until Sunday \nmorning when ridging builds over the forecast area. However, an \nupper level trough/low looks to remain across Northern Mexico and \nthe Four-Corners Region for the remainder of the long term period. A \nsurface low in association with this feature would allow southerly \nflow to continue across the Southern Plains, but meet easterly flow \nacross Kansas, forming a convergence zone. \n\nOverall, the long term period is favored to consist of moist \nconditions. On Friday and Saturday, shortwave troughs embedded in \nthe upper level southwesterly flow would allow several surface lows \nto track over the forecast area. Thunderstorms would be favored to \nform under this pattern. This is supported by NBM 24 hr guidance, \nwhich shows over a 40% chance for greater than 0.1 inches of \nprecipitation across nearly the entire forecast region both days, \nand as high as a 70-90% chance across portions of Eastern Colorado \nand Dundy County in Southwest Nebraska Friday. Storms are not \ncurrently expected to be severe during this period, as LREF guidance \nsuggests less than a 20% chance of seeing sfc-500mb shear greater \nthan 30 kts across most of the forecast area. However, it should be \nnoted that GFS model soundings show an inverted-V profile up to \nabout 3 km, associated with surface dewpoints around 50. This could \nsupport microburst potential, where winds could locally reach around \n60-70 mph with thunderstorms. However, this is not the favored \nscenario, as LREF guidance suggests a 60% chance or better that dew \npoints will reach above 60 F across the forecast area. As such, \nconfidence in severe weather occurring Friday or Saturday is less \nthan 5% at this time. High temperatures are currently forecast in \nthe 80s and lower 90s both Friday and Saturday. \n\nAs ridging builds in overhead Sunday and troughing develops across \nthe Four-Corners Region, conditions may start to dry out, as \nforecast relative humidities (RH) drop from the 50s and 60s on \nSunday to the 30s and 40s on Tuesday. This would be driven by a low \nto the southwest of the forecast area injecting hot, dry air into \nthe incoming southerly flow. Conditions look to remain moist enough \nfor thunderstorm activity to continue Sunday through Tuesday, but it \nis a point to keep in mind that drier conditions may be coming \ntoward the middle of next week. As for thunderstorm activity during \nthis period, it would be driven by southerly flow meeting easterly \nflow in Kansas, creating convergence in this zone. Again, LREF \nguidance is in support of thunderstorms remaining non-severe, \nshowing less than a 20% chance for wind shear greater than 30 \nkts across most of the forecast region all three days. If hotter\nand drier conditions can indeed develop, microburst potential \nmay become more of a concern. However, at this range, there is \nnot enough confidence to say that microbursts are likely. This \nwill be another development to watch as we go into next week. \nHigh temperatures are forecast in the 80s Sunday and Monday, and\nthen the upper 80s and lower 90s on Tuesday.",
